request.description:
From the project root, open MySQL using docker compose exec mysql mysql -uroot -p ecommerce_main. Enter the local password from the Docker configuration. Replace CUSTOMER_ID with customerCId from Variables, then run:

    DELIMITER //
    CREATE TRIGGER postman_fail_order_insert BEFORE INSERT ON orders
    FOR EACH ROW BEGIN
      IF NEW.user_id = CUSTOMER_ID THEN
        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Postman test: reject order insert';
      END IF;
    END//
    DELIMITER ;

Click Send and expect 500. Immediately remove the trigger in the same MySQL session:

    DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS postman_fail_order_insert;

Continue with the checks below.

Open PostgreSQL with docker compose exec inventory-db psql -U inventory_user -d ecommerce_inventory. Replace PRODUCT_ID with faultProductId from Variables and run:

    CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION postman_delay_reservation() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
    BEGIN
      IF NEW.product_id = PRODUCT_ID THEN PERFORM pg_sleep(4.5); END IF;
      RETURN NEW;
    END;
    $$;
    CREATE TRIGGER postman_delay_reservation BEFORE INSERT ON inventory_reservations
    FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION postman_delay_reservation();

The 4.5-second delay exceeds the default three-second gRPC timeout. If the application timeout differs, set a longer delay. Click Send and expect 503. Wait another three seconds before running the checks.

First remove the PostgreSQL delay trigger after the original call has settled:

DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS postman_delay_reservation ON inventory_reservations;
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS postman_delay_reservation();

Retry POST orders. The same stored reservation ID and basket are resumed. Expect exactly one order. Do not manually release its reservation.
